{"page":21918,"revision":1327936046,"ref":"o16","kind":"example","cue":"heading","strength":"strong","name":"Examples","section":"Examples","section_anchor":"Examples","span":[4414,8796],"text":"## Examples\nFor any group $G,$ the trivial subgroup $\\{ e \\}$ consisting of just the identity element of $G$ is always a normal subgroup of $G.$ Likewise, $G$ itself is always a normal subgroup of $G.$ (If these are the only normal subgroups, then $G$ is said to be simple.) Other named normal subgroups of an arbitrary group include the center of the group (the set of elements that commute with all other elements) and the commutator subgroup $[G,G].$ More generally, since conjugation is an isomorphism, any characteristic subgroup is a normal subgroup.\nIf $G$ is an abelian group then every subgroup $N$ of $G$ is normal, because $gN = \\{gn\\}_{n\\in N} = \\{ng\\}_{n\\in N} = Ng.$ More generally, for any group $G$, every subgroup of the center $Z(G)$ of $G$ is normal in $G$. (In the special case that $G$ is abelian, the center is all of $G$, hence the fact that all subgroups of an abelian group are normal.) A group that is not abelian but for which every subgroup is normal is called a Hamiltonian group.\nA concrete example of a normal subgroup is the subgroup $N = \\{(1), (123), (132)\\}$ of the symmetric group $S_3,$ consisting of the identity and both three-cycles. In particular, one can check that every coset of $N$ is either equal to $N$ itself or is equal to $(12)N = \\{ (12), (23), (13)\\}.$ On the other hand, the subgroup $H = \\{(1), (12)\\}$ is not normal in $S_3$ since $(123)H = \\{(123), (13) \\} \\neq \\{(123), (23) \\} = H(123).$ This illustrates the general fact that any subgroup $H \\leq G$ of index two is normal.\nAs an example of a normal subgroup within a matrix group, consider the general linear group $\\mathrm{GL}_n(\\mathbf{R})$ of all invertible $n\\times n$ matrices with real entries under the operation of matrix multiplication and its subgroup $\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})$ of all $n\\times n$ matrices of determinant 1 (the special linear group). To see why the subgroup $\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})$ is normal in $\\mathrm{GL}_n(\\mathbf{R})$, consider any matrix $X$ in $\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})$ and any invertible matrix $A$. Then using the two important identities $\\det(AB)=\\det(A)\\det(B)$ and $\\det(A^{-1})=\\det(A)^{-1}$, one has that $\\det(AXA^{-1}) = \\det(A) \\det(X) \\det(A)^{-1} = \\det(X) = 1$, and so $AXA^{-1} \\in \\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})$ as well. This means $\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})$ is closed under conjugation in $\\mathrm{GL}_n(\\mathbf{R})$, so it is a normal subgroup.\nIn the Rubik's Cube group, the subgroups consisting of operations which only affect the orientations of either the corner pieces or the edge pieces are normal.\nThe translation group is a normal subgroup of the Euclidean group in any dimension. This means: applying a rigid transformation, followed by a translation and then the inverse rigid transformation, has the same effect as a single translation. By contrast, the subgroup of all rotations about the origin is not a normal subgroup of the Euclidean group, as long as the dimension is at least 2: first translating, then rotating about the origin, and then translating back will typically not fix the origin and will therefore not have the same effect as a single rotation about the origin.","math":[{"form":"math","tex":"G,","source":"<math>G,</math>","span":[4443,4458]},{"form":"math","tex":"\\{ e \\}","source":"<math>\\{ e \\}</math>","span":[4480,4500]},{"form":"math","tex":"G","source":"<math>G</math>","span":[4544,4558]},{"form":"math","tex":"G.","source":"<math>G.</math>","span":[4590,4605]},{"form":"math","tex":"G","source":"<math>G</math>","span":[4617,4631]},{"form":"math","tex":"G.","source":"<math>G.</math>","span":[4670,4685]},{"form":"math","tex":"G","source":"<math>G</math>","span":[4732,4746]},{"form":"math","tex":"[G,G].","source":"<math>[G,G].</math>","span":[5014,5033]},{"form":"math","tex":"G","source":"<math>G</math>","span":[5219,5233]},{"form":"math","tex":"N","source":"<math>N</math>","span":[5278,5292]},{"form":"math","tex":"G","source":"<math>G</math>","span":[5296,5310]},{"form":"math","tex":"gN = \\{gn\\}_{n\\in N} = \\{ng\\}_{n\\in N} = Ng.","source":"<math>gN = \\{gn\\}_{n\\in N} = \\{ng\\}_{n\\in N} = Ng.</math>","span":[5330,5387]},{"form":"math","tex":"G","source":"<math>G</math>","span":[5418,5432]},{"form":"math","tex":"Z(G)","source":"<math>Z(G)</math>","span":[5467,5484]},{"form":"math","tex":"G","source":"<math>G</math>","span":[5488,5502]},{"form":"math","tex":"G","source":"<math>G</math>","span":[5516,5530]},{"form":"math","tex":"G","source":"<math>G</math>","span":[5558,5572]},{"form":"math","tex":"G","source":"<math>G</math>","span":[5606,5620]},{"form":"math","tex":"N = \\{(1), (123), (132)\\}","source":"<math>N = \\{(1), (123), (132)\\}</math>","span":[5872,5910]},{"form":"math","tex":"S_3,","source":"<math>S_3,</math>","span":[5938,5955]},{"form":"math","tex":"N","source":"<math>N</math>","span":[6056,6070]},{"form":"math","tex":"N","source":"<math>N</math>","span":[6090,6104]},{"form":"math","tex":"(12)N = \\{ (12), (23), (13)\\}.","source":"<math>(12)N = \\{ (12), (23), (13)\\}.</math>","span":[6127,6170]},{"form":"math","tex":"H = \\{(1), (12)\\}","source":"<math>H = \\{(1), (12)\\}</math>","span":[6204,6234]},{"form":"math","tex":"S_3","source":"<math>S_3</math>","span":[6252,6268]},{"form":"math","tex":"(123)H = \\{(123), (13) \\} \\neq \\{(123), (23) \\} = H(123).","source":"<math>(123)H = \\{(123), (13) \\} \\neq \\{(123), (23) \\} = H(123).</math>","span":[6275,6345]},{"form":"math","tex":"H \\leq G","source":"<math>H \\leq G</math>","span":[6436,6457]},{"form":"math","tex":"\\mathrm{GL}_n(\\mathbf{R})","source":"<math>\\mathrm{GL}_n(\\mathbf{R})</math>","span":[6607,6645]},{"form":"math","tex":"n\\times n","source":"<math>n\\times n</math>","span":[6664,6686]},{"form":"math","tex":"\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})","source":"<math>\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})</math>","span":[6776,6814]},{"form":"math","tex":"n\\times n","source":"<math>n\\times n</math>","span":[6822,6844]},{"form":"math","tex":"\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})","source":"<math>\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})</math>","span":[6958,6996]},{"form":"math","tex":"\\mathrm{GL}_n(\\mathbf{R})","source":"<math>\\mathrm{GL}_n(\\mathbf{R})</math>","span":[7010,7048]},{"form":"math","tex":"X","source":"<math>X</math>","span":[7070,7084]},{"form":"math","tex":"\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})","source":"<math>\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})</math>","span":[7088,7126]},{"form":"math","tex":"A","source":"<math>A</math>","span":[7153,7167]},{"form":"math","tex":"\\det(AB)=\\det(A)\\det(B)","source":"<math>\\det(AB)=\\det(A)\\det(B)</math>","span":[7210,7246]},{"form":"math","tex":"\\det(A^{-1})=\\det(A)^{-1}","source":"<math>\\det(A^{-1})=\\det(A)^{-1}</math>","span":[7251,7289]},{"form":"math","tex":"\\det(AXA^{-1}) = \\det(A) \\det(X) \\det(A)^{-1} = \\det(X) = 1","source":"<math>\\det(AXA^{-1}) = \\det(A) \\det(X) \\det(A)^{-1} = \\det(X) = 1</math>","span":[7304,7376]},{"form":"math","tex":"AXA^{-1} \\in \\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})","source":"<math>AXA^{-1} \\in \\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})</math>","span":[7385,7436]},{"form":"math","tex":"\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})","source":"<math>\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbf{R})</math>","span":[7459,7497]},{"form":"math","tex":"\\mathrm{GL}_n(\\mathbf{R})","source":"<math>\\mathrm{GL}_n(\\mathbf{R})</math>","span":[7529,7567]}],"links":[{"title":"Simple group"},{"title":"Center (group theory)"},{"title":"Commutator subgroup"},{"title":"Characteristic subgroup"},{"title":"Abelian group"},{"title":"Hamiltonian group"},{"title":"Symmetric group"},{"title":"Index of a subgroup"},{"title":"Matrix group"},{"title":"General linear group"},{"title":"Determinant (mathematics)"},{"title":"Special linear group"},{"title":"Rubik's Cube group"},{"title":"Translation group"},{"title":"Euclidean group"},{"title":"Rotation"}],"citations":["o17","o18","o19","o20","o21","o22","o23","o24"],"hash":"sha256:2dd6b9213619c092f19afa5123ed9b1238228a0ca0223997d181ae55a8d083d0","extractor":"m2/v0.3.8"}
